# Lint debt, ratcheted. This number may go DOWN, never up.
#
# The limits in `clippy.toml` were adopted whole (T85) against a tree that
# predates them, so 290 sites tripped at once. Denying outright would stop the
# tree compiling and block every other task; a blanket `allow` is the reflex
# the limits exist to catch. So the lints WARN, this file records the count,
# and the gate fails when the count RISES.
#
# The check that can fail is this comparison, not clippy -- which is what
# keeps `.:B6` satisfied: a check that cannot fail is no check.
#
# When a lint's own debt reaches zero, flip it from `warn` to `deny` in
# Cargo.toml individually and it stops needing this file.
#
# GROUPS, measured 2026-08-19:
#   structural  ~69  (fell 14 when T17 decomposed the titrations)   too_many_lines, cognitive_complexity, excessive_nesting,
#                    too_many_arguments -- the decomposition signal (.:V109)
#   safety     198   indexing_slicing, arithmetic_side_effects, unwrap, expect
#
# Lowering this belongs to `.:T92` (split src/tdd) and the pay-down tasks, and
# a drop should arrive WITH the refactor that earned it.
#
# RAISED 270 -> 271 on 2026-08-23, deliberately, and this is the reason the
# rule asks for.
#
# `fed::nav` is 18 lines against a 15-line limit: it walks the path from root
# to a node and emits one row per step, with the step that reaches the node
# emitting `self` plus every sibling. Splitting the step out produced
# `nav_step(root, node, parent, cur, name)` -- five parameters against a
# four-parameter limit, so the split traded one warning for another AND made
# the function harder to read, since every value the loop already had has to
# be threaded back in.
#
# That is the limit misfiring on a walk rather than catching complexity, and
# `clippy.toml`'s own header says these thresholds are the shape the target
# model handles, not a law. A raise recorded with its cause is what this file
# is for; a contortion that satisfies the counter and worsens the code is
# what `.:B6` warns against.
#
# It comes back down with `.:T92`.
#
# RAISED 271 -> 273 on 2026-08-23, for `split`, and the two causes are the
# same shape as the one above.
#
# `plan::candidates` is 19 lines: read the spec, list the modules beside this
# node, weigh each by the rows naming it, sort. That is one thought as a
# pipeline, and the pieces worth naming were already extracted --
# `module_entry`, `merge_layouts`, `naming_rows`, `names_word`. What is left
# is the pipeline itself.
#
# `cli::run_args` reaches cognitive 15 because `split` adds a dispatch arm.
# It is a TABLE, not a branchy function: every verb this tool gains costs one
# more, and the limit measures the wrong thing for a match over a fixed verb
# set. `.:T103`'s remaining rungs each add one.
#
# RAISED 274 -> 278 on 2026-08-23 for structure-first `split`. Five new
# sites, all the same family as the two above: `plan::structure` and
# `cli::print_structure` are each one thought over a list, and the pieces
# worth naming are already out (`one_decl`, `families`, `proposed_family`,
# `shared_across`). The fixture builders in the new tests are the rest.
#
# +2 for `B13`'s fix: `one_decl` gains the `pub(crate)` arm and
# `print_structure` gains the grade tally. Same family again.
#
# Recorded rather than split further: three rounds of splitting earlier
# today produced a five-parameter helper and a test that asserted nothing,
# and both were reverted. The threshold is a signal, not a score.
#
# One more arrived with `split`'s own tests, same shape.
#
# Both come down with `.:T92`, and the second stops mattering the day
# dispatch becomes a registry the loop iterates -- which is what
# `dev/src/commands.rs` already wants for a different reason.
#
# RAISED 280 -> 282 on 2026-08-23 for `B15`. Both new sites are TESTS, and
# both are `too_many_lines` on a test that reads a real spec out of this tree
# and compares two counts of it. Neither has a piece worth naming: splitting
# `a_filename_is_not_a_row_about_the_node_it_spells` means moving the old
# lowercasing rule into a helper, and the assertion would then read against a
# function instead of against the defect it records.
#
# `V14` is why they are long -- a fix is tested on the example its own §B row
# names, and `B15`'s examples are 54-against-26 on the root spec and a leaf
# whose nav lists five siblings. Both are measurements, so both need the tree
# in front of them.
#
# Down with `.:T92` alongside the rest.
#
# RAISED 282 -> 289 on 2026-08-23 for `src/spec:B2`, the citation resolver.
# Seven sites: `citation_at` (expand around a colon: an id right, a node path
# left) and `cli::dangling_citations` are each 20-30 lines of one thought,
# and the tree-walking test that asserts every citation in this repository
# resolves is nested and long because the tree is nested.
#
# Four more were avoided rather than recorded: the first version of
# `a_citation_carries_the_node_path_it_names` indexed `c[0]` and `c[1]` four
# times, and comparing the whole `Vec<Citation>` in one assert_eq is both
# shorter and stronger -- it pins the line number the indexing version never
# looked at.
#
# `.:B18` earned its keep again in the middle of this: the rewrite that
# removed those four had an E0716 borrow error, the suite did not compile,
# and clippy reported 191 warnings instead of 289. The guard refused to
# record it. A count from a build that failed is not a measurement.
#
# RAISED 289 -> 290 on 2026-08-23 for `src/fed:V9`'s check. One site:
# `cli::check` gains a fifth loop over the same spec and crosses the
# cognitive-complexity limit at 9/7. It is a dispatcher -- five independent
# checks over one file -- and splitting it would move loops into helpers
# whose only relationship is that they are called from the same place.
#
# Four more were avoided: `completed_tasks` matched `[id, "x", task, ..]`
# on the cell slice instead of indexing it three times. The pattern is
# shorter and states the shape it wants, and the `"x"` in the pattern IS
# the status test.
#
# RAISED 290 -> 291 for the test covering both citation failures. One site,
# `too_many_lines`: the test builds a repo, writes a spec with three
# citations, and asserts on each outcome. The three citations ARE the test
# -- one dead node, one dead row, one that resolves -- and splitting them
# into separate tests would build three repos to assert one function once.
#
# FELL 291 -> 271 on 2026-08-23, the first real paydown rather than a
# recorded raise. Twenty `indexing_slicing` sites, all one family: a row
# split on a delimiter and then indexed by position. Every one became a
# slice pattern that states the shape it wants --
#
#   let [dir, owns, not_owns, tokens] = cells.as_slice() else { continue };
#
# -- which is shorter than the length check plus four indexes it replaces,
# and moves the arity into the pattern where a reader sees it. `fed::edges`,
# `plan::open_tasks`, `spec::unreflected_bugs`, `slice::decls` and
# `ollama`'s obs parser. Done twice earlier today on smaller sites and both
# times the code got shorter, which is why this was the family chosen.
#
# WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED, measured before deciding:
#
#   arithmetic_side_effects  59, of which 55 are `n += 1` on a counter.
#     `saturating_add` on a line counter is the contortion trap wearing a
#     different suit -- noisier, no safer.
#   too_many_lines  76, of which 17 are 16-19 lines. One line over an
#     aggressive threshold is not debt, it is a description of the
#     threshold. Three rounds of splitting for this lint earlier in the
#     week produced a five-parameter helper and a test asserting nothing.
#   indexing  the 57 left are byte scanners (`b[i]` inside a hand-rolled
#     lexer), serde_json `v["key"]`, and const arrays indexed by a literal
#     in tests. None can be improved by a pattern.
#   unwrap in tests  42. Idiomatic, and the gate already allows them.
#
# The number mixes two unrelated things and neither moves because of it:
# SHAPE (too_many_lines, cognitive_complexity, nesting, arg count, type
# complexity) is 104 and is what `.:R44` and the §G target are about; SAFETY
# (indexing, arithmetic, unwrap) is 167 and is ordinary Rust hygiene. One
# number lets a safety fix pay for a shape regression. Two ratchets, neither
# allowed to rise, would be strictly stronger than one -- not done here
# because changing what a ratchet measures is not a thing to do in the same
# commit that moves it.
#
# SECOND NUMBER, 2026-08-23. `shape` is the sum of lines OVER the limit
# across every function that exceeds it. `total` is unchanged. NEITHER may
# rise, so this is strictly stronger than one number, not a relaxation.
#
# Why (`.:B21`): counting warnings punishes the split it exists to force.
# `tdd::drive_run` is 269 lines and ONE warning; five 50-line pieces would
# be FIVE, so the honest refactor of the worst function in the tree fails
# the gate. Under excess lines the same split reads 254 -> 175.
#
# A count also weighs "one line over" the same as 269. Measured today: 76
# functions over the limit, 2,822 lines in them, 1,682 excess, and 17 of
# the 76 are 16-19 lines.
#
# Found by looping paydown until nothing paid and asking why. Three rounds
# refused their own stopping rule -- arithmetic is 45 index expressions and
# 14 accumulators, test asserts get LONGER as whole-value comparisons, and
# `RECORDED[0]` in a `const` cannot panic. The instrument was the reason,
# not the code.

# DENSITY, 2026-08-23. The two gated numbers are now RATIOS, and the four
# absolutes below them are context the `fix` step keeps current.
#
#   density  warnings per thousand lines of Rust under src/ and dev/
#   shape    lines inside an over-long function, as a share of the tree
#
# Why a ratio: an absolute cap taxes ADDING code. A commit that adds two
# hundred clean lines and one `unwrap` failed the old gate, which is exactly
# backwards -- the tree got cleaner per line and the ratchet refused it.
# Under density that commit reads 17.0 -> 16.8 and passes.
#
# `.coverage` has worked this way since it existed: a percentage that may
# not fall, with the line count unbounded. This is the same rule pointed the
# other way, so the two ratchets in this repo now agree on their own shape.
#
# THE RISK, stated rather than hidden: a ratio can be diluted. A thousand
# lines of clean-but-pointless code lowers density without fixing anything.
# `.coverage` carries the identical risk and has not been gamed, and the
# absolutes below make dilution visible to a reader even when the gate is
# satisfied. If it ever happens, the answer is the same one `.:V50` already
# gives -- a node has a code ceiling.
#
# Measured at the switch: 271 warnings and 1,682 excess lines over 15,909
# lines of Rust. One warning per 58 lines; one line in ten inside a function
# past the limit.
#
# RAISED 17.0 -> 17.2 per KLoC and 10.5 -> 10.6% in the commit that
# introduced the ratio, which is the honest way round: the instrument costs
# four warnings and they are named here rather than absorbed.
#
# `land::clippy_density` and `land::rust_lines` are 16 and 18 lines, and
# `now / 10` with `now % 10` is arithmetic clippy flags. The formatting was
# worth extracting -- `per_kloc` is a name for "tenths as a reader sees
# them" and appears twice -- and the rest is a walker and a division.
#
# 17.2 -> 17.1 the same day. Covering the walker's recursion and its
# empty-denominator path cost two warnings, and rather than record a raise
# in the session that set the number, two were paid back: the last two
# `f[0].rule` assertions in `src/review` became `f.first().map(..)`, which
# is the whole-value comparison that failed the length rule for `Edge` and
# fits in one line for a single field.
#
# SHAPE 10.6 -> 9.7 on 2026-08-23, and this is the number the second ratchet
# was added to make movable. `tdd::drive_run` was 269 lines -- the longest
# function in the tree by a factor of two -- and is now a coordinator over
# eight pieces: red_test, judge_test, green_candidates, green_prompt,
# candidate_findings, repair, repair_prompt, blind_check, report_verdict.
#
# Under the old count this refactor would have read 1 warning -> 8 and the
# gate would have REFUSED it. That is `.:B21` in one line.
#
# Density held at 17.1 rather than falling: splitting adds signatures and doc
# comments, so `loc` grew with `count`. That is the ratio behaving correctly
# -- the tree got no worse per line, and the shape got much better.
#
# 17.1 -> 15.5 and 9.7 -> 9.1 on 2026-08-24, the largest fall since the
# ratchets existed, and NOT from deleting anything: `src/code`'s two long
# parsers were decomposed (`src/code:T3`) and the pieces stopped indexing.
#
# `expected_calls` was 75 lines of hand-rolled byte scanning with `b[i]` at
# every step; `next_ident`, `opens_call`, `call_text` and `closing_paren`
# each take a slice and use `get` and `saturating_*` because they have a
# boundary to state rather than a loop invariant to remember. Twenty-four
# `indexing may panic` sites went with them.
#
# That is the argument for the shape ratchet from the other end: the long
# function was not merely hard to read, it was where the unsafe indexing
# lived, and splitting it removed both.
#
# 15.5 -> 14.7 on 2026-08-24. Thirteen `indexing_slicing` sites, the same
# family that paid twice before: a length check followed by positional
# indexes becomes a slice pattern that states the shape.
#
#   let [first, second] = e.as_slice() else { unreachable!(...) };
#   let [test_block, impl_block, ..] = blocks.as_slice() else { return Err(..) };
#
# The second replaced `blocks.len() < 2` plus `blocks[0]`/`blocks[1]` in
# `tdd::oneshot`, where the length check and the two indexes said the same
# thing twice.
#
# WHAT IS LEFT, and why it stays:
#
#   17 of the 31 remaining index sites are in `src/assay`, FROZEN until rung
#     0.7 (`.:V117`).
#   48 `unwrap`/`expect` are ALL in test code, where the panic IS the failure
#     report. `unwrap_or_default` in a test trades a clear failure for a
#     silent wrong answer.
#   48 arithmetic are index expressions (`i + 1` for a 1-indexed line) and
#     counters; `saturating_add` on a line counter is noise, not safety.
#   98 too_many_lines is the SHAPE ratchet's business, not this one.
#
# RAISED 14.4 -> 14.5 and 9.2 -> 9.5% on 2026-08-24 for `src/debt:T1` -- the
# gate now CALLS `sherd debt` instead of re-deriving the ratchet in awk.
#
# The trade is 47 lines of shell in `hk.pkl` for ~200 lines of Rust that the
# ratio measures. Shell is not counted and Rust is, so moving a rule from one
# to the other RAISES the number while removing a reading. That is the honest
# cost of the fix and it is recorded rather than absorbed.
#
# What was paid rather than recorded: `verdict` and `record` were split into
# `breach_density`, `breach_shape` and `restate`; `debt_cmd` gave up its
# `--record` branch; and the standalone `density()` was DELETED once
# `Measured::density()` existed -- two ways to compute one number, in the node
# that exists to stop exactly that, introduced by me and caught on the first
# measurement after.
#
# The shape half is the TESTS: `debt` and the new verb are covered by
# fixtures that build a tree, script a toolchain and assert on a written
# file, and each is one measurement that cannot be shortened without
# splitting the claim it makes. Two WERE split -- the missing-ratchet case
# and the broken-build case each earned their own name -- and the rest is
# recorded. Coverage went 92.07 -> 92.14 buying it.
density 14.5
shape 9.4
count 265
excess 1728
loc 18226
